/**
\page page_grid_statistics Grid statistics and other global mesh information


## Grid statistics 

Besides the actual vertex/face/cell data your solver attaches to the mesh,
Peano's core continuously tracks a small set of statistics about the mesh
itself while it traverses it: how many cells are refined vs unrefined, how
long the mesh has been unchanged, how fine the finest cells currently are,
and so forth. These data live in @ref peano4::grid::GridStatistics.


### Dataflow

- Every peano4::grid::Spacetree accumulates its own
  @ref peano4::grid::GridStatistics object while it descends through its cells
  during a traversal.
- Each rank typically owns several spacetrees at once. All of them are
  collected within @ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet, the singleton that
  owns and traverses all of a rank's local trees.
- At the end of a traversal, the @ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet reduces
  the local statistics into one @ref peano4::grid::GridStatistics object -
  a plain, in-process C++ `operator+` (src/peano4/grid/grid.cpp) that just
  adds up the per-tree counters field by field. No MPI is involved in this
  step.
- **This is where it stops.** The resulting @ref peano4::grid::GridStatistics
  object is never combined across MPI ranks: there is no reduce, no
  send/receive, no global master collecting everyone else's numbers, despite
  what the diagram in an earlier revision of this page implied. If you run
  with several ranks, each one accumulates and reports its own, independent
  view of the mesh - see "Ranks act independently" below.

\dot
digraph GridStatisticsDataflow {
  rankdir=LR
  fontname="Helvetica"
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10]
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9]

  subgraph cluster_rank {
    label     = "one rank"
    fontname  = "Helvetica"
    fontsize  = 10
    style     = dashed

    Spacetree1 [label="Spacetree #1"]
    Spacetree2 [label="Spacetree #2"]
    SpacetreeN [label="…"]
    SpacetreeSet [label="SpacetreeSet", fillcolor=lightgrey]
    RankStats [label="GridStatistics\n(this rank)"]

    Spacetree1 -> SpacetreeSet [label="1: accumulate\nwhile descending"]
    Spacetree2 -> SpacetreeSet
    SpacetreeN -> SpacetreeSet [label="2: owns/traverses\nall local trees", style=dashed]
    SpacetreeSet -> RankStats [label="3: reduce at end\nof traversal\n(local operator+, no MPI)"]
  }
}
\enddot


### Ranks act independently

Nothing above ever crosses a rank boundary. @ref peano4::DefaultStepReport::reportStep()
- and exaparty's GlobalState.template.cpp, despite "Global" being in its name -
simply logs whatever @ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getGridStatistics()
returns *on that rank*. Run the same code with, say, three MPI ranks, and you
get three independent log lines, one per rank, each describing only that
rank's share of the mesh - never a sum, and never anything resembling a
combined, global view.

\dot
digraph GridStatisticsIndependence {
  rankdir=LR
  fontname="Helvetica"
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10]
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9]

  subgraph cluster_rank0 {
    label    = "rank 0"
    fontname = "Helvetica"
    fontsize = 10
    style    = dashed
    SpacetreeSet0 [label="SpacetreeSet", fillcolor=lightgrey]
    Stats0        [label="GridStatistics\n(rank 0)"]
    Log0          [label="logInfo(...)", shape=note, fillcolor=lightyellow]
    SpacetreeSet0 -> Stats0 -> Log0
  }

  subgraph cluster_rank1 {
    label    = "rank 1"
    fontname = "Helvetica"
    fontsize = 10
    style    = dashed
    SpacetreeSet1 [label="SpacetreeSet", fillcolor=lightgrey]
    Stats1        [label="GridStatistics\n(rank 1)"]
    Log1          [label="logInfo(...)", shape=note, fillcolor=lightyellow]
    SpacetreeSet1 -> Stats1 -> Log1
  }

  subgraph cluster_rankN {
    label    = "rank …"
    fontname = "Helvetica"
    fontsize = 10
    style    = dashed
    SpacetreeSetN [label="SpacetreeSet", fillcolor=lightgrey]
    StatsN        [label="GridStatistics\n(rank …)"]
    LogN          [label="logInfo(...)", shape=note, fillcolor=lightyellow]
    SpacetreeSetN -> StatsN -> LogN
  }
}
\enddot

The absence of any edge between the three clusters above is the point:
nothing in this dataflow talks to another rank. If you need a genuinely
global number, you have to compute it yourself (e.g. your own explicit
MPI_Allreduce over whichever field you need), or lean on the one thing in
the codebase that already does this, narrowly - see "Load balancing
information" below.


### A typical use

The information on each rank or tree can be read out through the
spacetree set:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
peano4::grid::GridStatistics stats =
  peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getInstance().getGridStatistics();
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A common place, where this is used, is the default step reporter
@ref peano4::DefaultStepReport::reportStep() which you can use as a
starting point for our own use of the stats. Remember, per the above, that
this is always the *local* view - the exact same call made on another rank
will in general return different numbers:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
const auto stats = peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getInstance().getGridStatistics();

logInfo(
  "finishTimeStep()",
  "min mesh size="    << tarch::la::max(stats.getMinH()) <<
  ", #local cells=("  << stats.getNumberOfLocalUnrefinedCells()  << "/" << stats.getNumberOfLocalRefinedCells()  << ")" <<
  ", #remote cells=(" << stats.getNumberOfRemoteUnrefinedCells() << "/" << stats.getNumberOfRemoteRefinedCells() << ")"
);
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~



## Load balancing information

Unlike the reporting path above, @ref toolbox::loadbalancing::Statistics::updateGlobalView()
does perform a real, MPI-level reduction - but a much narrower one than
"reduce the GridStatistics object" would suggest. It pulls exactly one
scalar out of the local GridStatistics -
peano4::grid::GridStatistics::getNumberOfLocalUnrefinedCells() - copies it
into a plain `int`, and feeds that into a non-blocking
`MPI_Iallreduce(..., MPI_SUM, ...)` to obtain a genuinely global cell count
(src/toolbox/loadbalancing/Statistics.cpp). A handful of other scalars are
Allreduce'd the same way alongside it: the global number of splits, the
global number of trees, how many ranks currently have load balancing
switched on, and the maximum number of trees held by any one rank.

This global view exists exclusively to steer the load balancer's own
rebalancing decisions. It is not exposed as part of GridStatistics itself,
and it does not feed back into the per-rank reporting path described above -
the two remain entirely separate.


*/
